package docs

import (
	"github.com/filipejohansson/vane/core"

	"vane-page/src/components"
)

func Lifecycle() core.Node {
	core.Head(func() core.HeadConfig {
		return core.HeadConfig{
			Title:       "Lifecycle | Vane Docs",
			Description: "How Vane mounts components, reruns effects, disposes cleanup, and tears down route scopes.",
		}
	})

	items := lifecycleTOCItems

	return (
		<div className="docs-page">
			<article className="docs-article">
				{docsHeader(
					"Advanced",
					"Lifecycle",
					"The execution model for components, Effects, cleanup, and route disposal in Vane.",
				)}

				<h2 className="docs-h2" id="mount">Mount and setup</h2>
				<p className="docs-p">
					A component function runs once when it mounts. That setup phase creates the DOM,
					registers any local signals, and installs the Effects and cleanup hooks that belong
					to that component scope.
				</p>
				<p className="docs-p">
					This is different from a re-render model: Vane does not call the component again just
					because a value changed. Instead, the component stays mounted and only the specific
					bindings and Effects that read the changed signal are scheduled to re-run.
				</p>

				<h2 className="docs-h2" id="effects">Effects and reruns</h2>
				<p className="docs-p">
					An Effect runs immediately when created, then re-runs only when one of the signals it
					reads changes. The effect tracks its dependencies automatically during the run and
					re-registers them on each subsequent run.
				</p>
				{components.CodeBlock("effect.go", `core.Effect(func() func() {
    count := count.Get()
    console.Log("count changed:", count)
    return nil
})`)}
				<p className="docs-p">
					If the Effect reads no signal, it has no reactive dependency and does not re-run on
					state changes. In that case, its cleanup only runs when the component is disposed.
				</p>

				<h2 className="docs-h2" id="cleanup">Cleanup and disposal</h2>
				<p className="docs-p">
					Cleanup is part of the Effect lifecycle. When an Effect is about to re-run because a
					dependency changed, Vane calls the previous cleanup first. This gives you a chance to
					detach listeners, stop timers, cancel subscriptions, and release temporary resources.
				</p>
				<p className="docs-p">
					The same cleanup path also runs on unmount. Any <code>core.OnDispose</code> callbacks
					registered in the component scope are invoked as the scope is torn down.
				</p>

				<h2 className="docs-h2" id="routes">Route disposal</h2>
				<p className="docs-p">
					Route changes dispose the previous route scope. That means any Effects, subscriptions,
					or other resources created while the old route was active are cleaned up before the new
					route becomes active.
				</p>
				<p className="docs-p">
					In practice, this is the lifecycle boundary that keeps route-local state from leaking
					across navigations.
				</p>

				<h2 className="docs-h2" id="signals">Signal lifetime</h2>
				<p className="docs-p">
					Signals created inside a component belong to that component's lifetime. They are
					disposable with the same scope that owns the component's Effects, so they stop being
					active when the component is disposed or the route scope is torn down.
				</p>
				<p className="docs-p">
					This is the key mental model: the scope owns the reactive resources that were created
					for that node of the UI, and the cleanup lifecycle is what tears them down.
				</p>

				{pageNav("lifecycle")}
			</article>
			{toc(items)}
		</div>
	)
}
